#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
#  virtuoso-export-embedding-model
#
#  Export a HuggingFace sentence-embedding model to the ONNX form Virtuoso's
#  embedding path expects.
#
#  A stock HuggingFace ONNX export cannot be used here, for two reasons that
#  have nothing to do with each other:
#
#    * it takes three inputs (input_ids, attention_mask, token_type_ids) and
#      the out-of-process helper binds exactly two;
#    * its output is last_hidden_state -- one vector per token, not one per
#      sentence.  Pooling happens in the library, outside the graph.
#
#  So this script wraps the transformer in pooling (and normalisation, if the
#  model asks for it), folds token_type_ids away as a constant, and exports a
#  graph that takes input_ids and attention_mask and returns pooled_output.
#
#  The helper tokenizes in C, with one of two tokenizers: WordPiece (BERT,
#  vocab.txt) or byte-level BPE (RoBERTa and GPT-2, vocab.json with merges.txt
#  beside it).  The tokenizer is read from the model too, and the files it
#  needs are copied beside the export.
#
#  The pooling mode is READ from the model's own sentence-transformers
#  configuration, never assumed.  This matters more than it looks: pooling a
#  CLS-pooled model with mean does not fail, it returns plausible embeddings
#  that rank badly.  An early MiniLM export of ours had that defect --
#  correcting it moved FAQ hit@1 from 33% to 56%.  A tool that assumed mean
#  would hand that same defect to everyone with a CLS-pooled model.
#
#  Usage:
#      virtuoso-export-embedding-model <model> <outdir> [options]
#      virtuoso-export-embedding-model <model> /dev/null --dry-run
#
#  <model> is a HuggingFace id or a local directory.
#
#  --dry-run prints what would be exported and exits.  It reads configuration
#  only, and deliberately does not import torch, so it works anywhere python3
#  does.
#
#  --bf16-weights stores every weight whose values are all exact bfloat16
#  numbers as bfloat16, behind a Cast that ONNX Runtime folds back to the same
#  fp32 weights when it loads the model.  It loses nothing by construction and
#  the tool checks the outputs are identical.  A model published in bf16 and
#  exported in fp32 -- the bundled granite one -- halves; a model trained in
#  fp32 has almost no such weights and comes out as it was.
